<title>USA: Late freeze would damage slow U.S. corn -- USDA.</title>
<headline>Late freeze would damage slow U.S. corn -- USDA.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-09-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. corn is so late this year that there will be crop damage even if a killing freeze arrives late, USDA meteorologist Mark Brusberg said Tuesday.</p>
<p>&quot;They're not going to be able to avoid completely any damage, even with a very late frost,&quot; Brusberg told USDA radio.</p>
<p>Overall, only 37 percent of corn had reached the mature stage, against 56 percent in a normal year, with Ohio the furthest behind, Brusberg said.</p>
<p>He also said winter wheat planting was late because of wet weather, especially in Kansas and Oklahoma.</p>
<p>And wet weather and insects were affecting the quality of the cotton crop, he said.</p>
<p>-- Washington Commodities Desk (202) 898-8394</p>
</text>
